          %p
            This might follow from constructing it using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+gluing"} double gluing
            and orthogonality.
          %h2#construction_as_a_comma_double_category Construction as a Comma Double Category
          %p
            We can define arity spaces by a variation on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+gluing"} double gluing
            construction.
          %p
            Define a double category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Orth
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Orth
            of orthogonalities 1. Objects are relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot \subseteq X \times Y
            2. A vertical morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,Y_1,\perp_1)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2, Y_2, \perp_2)
            exists when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_1
            are orthogonal subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_2
            respectively. 3. A horizontal morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,Y_1,\perp_1)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2,Y_2,\perp_2)
            is a pair of a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_* : X_1 \to X_2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* : Y_2 \to Y_1
            4. A square from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            exists when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
            is the restriction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_*
            and similarly for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^*
          %p
            Then the (2-)category of arity spaces can be defined as the comma double category (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
